course: dictionary
lesson: 5869
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. Look at the words around the word to find out which meaning the writer means. Mandarin is a good example: it can be a fruit, a language or a person in history.
explain: Think about «peel a mandarin» and «mandarin oranges». Peeling and oranges are clues that mandarin means a small, sweet orange fruit that is easy to peel. Clementines and satsumas are kinds of mandarins.
explain: A capital M changes the meaning. «Mandarin Chinese is the language with the most native speakers on Earth.» Here Mandarin is the name of a language, the main form of Chinese, and the words «speak» and «Chinese» are your clues.
explain: In history lessons, a mandarin was an important official in old China. Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If the sentence still makes sense, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: A word with more than one meaning is sometimes called a multiple-meaning word.
fact: «Mandarin» means a small, sweet orange fruit that is easy to peel.
fact: «Mandarin» with a capital M is the main form of Chinese, the language spoken by the most people as a first language.
fact: In history, a mandarin was an important official in old China.
fact: Clues such as «peel», «speak» or «Chinese» help you decide which meaning is right.
exercise: «Peel a mandarin and share it with your sister.» What is a mandarin here? | *A small, sweet orange fruit that is easy to peel | An important official in old China | A type of Chinese cooking pot | A large, sour lemon
exercise: «She can speak Mandarin fluently.» What is Mandarin here? | *A language | A kind of tall bird | A village near the sea | A fruit with a thick green skin
exercise: Which clue tells you that «Mandarin» in «Mandarin Chinese» is a language and not a fruit? | The word sweet | The word lunchbox | The word peel | *The word Chinese next to it
test: «Mandarin oranges are a popular lunchbox fruit.» What does mandarin mean in this sentence? | An important official in old China | A type of bread | A language spoken in China | *A small, sweet orange fruit
test: In a history book, a mandarin was described as an educated official who ran the empire. What does mandarin mean here? | A fruit that grows on trees | A kind of Chinese dance | *An important official in old China | A sweet made from sugar
exam: Read «Mandarin Chinese uses tones and is written with characters.» What does Mandarin mean here, and which clue helped you decide? | A fruit, because of the word lunchbox | A sweet, because of the word sugar | An official, because of the word empire | *A language, because of the words Chinese, tones and characters
